<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spratly Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spratly Islands are a scattered group of more than a hundred small islands, reefs and atolls in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. They are claimed wholly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and in part by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, all of which except Brunei occupy some features. Chinese and Vietnamese forces clashed at Johnson South Reef in 1988, and since 2013 China has built artificial islands with airstrips on several reefs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spratly-islands.jpg" alt="Spratly Islands" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Spiridon Manoliu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spratly_Is_since_NalGeoMaps.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Spratly Islands are an archipelago in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>, off the coasts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, Malaysia and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. They are made up of islands, islets, cays and more than 100 reefs, sometimes grouped in submerged old atolls. They are named after Richard Spratly, a 19th-century British whaling captain who sighted Spratly Island in 1843. The naturally occurring land totals less than 200 hectares, spread over hundreds of square kilometres of sea, and the islands are cays, sand islands formed on old degraded and submerged coral reefs. Their northeast part, known as Dangerous Ground, has low islands, sunken reefs and degraded atolls rising abruptly from depths greater than 1,000 metres. This makes navigation hazardous.</p><p>Geologically the reefs and banks of biogenic carbonate lie on the crests of submarine ridges formed of uplifted fault blocks. Almost no arable land exists, and very few islands have a permanent supply of drinkable water. Resources include fish, guano, oil and natural gas, and the islands lie near major shipping lanes. In 1939 they were coral islets mostly inhabited by seabirds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The nearby Tabon Caves on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palawan">Palawan</a> hold evidence of human presence dating back nearly 50,000 years, and by 2000 BCE Austronesian peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> had established the maritime jade road. Seafarers of the Sa Huỳnh culture, forebears of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> people, may have passed through the Spratly Islands on their way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> between 600 BCE and 3 BCE.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> work Zhu fan zhi by Zhao Rugua uses names for reefs and sands that some interpret as the Paracels and the Spratly Islands respectively, though different authors read these names differently. A Ming text, Haiyu, uses Wanli Changsha for the Spratly Islands. The Qing continued to include the area on maps compiled between 1724 and 1817 without officially claiming jurisdiction. A European chart of 1758 left the Dangerous Ground largely blank, since it had not been surveyed. In the 19th century Europeans found Chinese fishermen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a> staying on the islands for part of each year, and in 1877 the British made the first modern legal claims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The archipelago is claimed in whole or part by several states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> each claim it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and Malaysia claim parts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> claims an exclusive economic zone in the southeast. Of about 45 islands, cays, reefs and shoals that are occupied, all have structures held by military forces of Malaysia, China, Taiwan, the Philippines and Vietnam. In January 2013 the Philippines began arbitration proceedings against China under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-convention-on-the-law-of-the-sea">United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea</a>. The surrounding waters hold over 600 coral reefs and 2,927 recorded marine species.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spratly Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Border fighting with China continued through the 1980s, notably around Vi Xuyen in Ha Giang province, and in 1988 Chinese ships killed 64 Vietnamese sailors in the Johnson South Reef skirmish in the <b>Spratly Islands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li></ul></section>
